09:59.484 --> 10:10.190
[SPEAKER_01]: themselves, it's a fine line to walk. And it's important to, to. keep an eye on that bar and say, you know what, that bar is there, that bar is high,

10:11.092 --> 10:23.247
[SPEAKER_01]: but I don't need to meet that bar today. An example I would like to give from my life shortly before I got into paragliding was that I wanted to

10:23.287 --> 10:32.893
[SPEAKER_01]: become a leader inside of a community that actually didn't even exist yet in my city of Toronto. I was 23, it was 2003, and I was taking a

10:32.933 --> 10:41.056
[SPEAKER_01]: leadership course. And so I had to organize the event as part of my homework. And I realized I don't want to be a leader in the photography

10:41.096 --> 10:49.404
[SPEAKER_01]: community, in the fashion photography community. That was what I was at the time. I don't want to be a leader amongst my friend group, my friends

10:49.424 --> 10:56.791
[SPEAKER_01]: from high school, which is still the people I hung out with at the time. I want to meet more people that are into skateboarding, that are into

10:56.851 --> 11:04.318
[SPEAKER_01]: longboarding. And I organized an event in Toronto called the Board Meeting. And it was tongue in cheek because essentially it was a huge

11:04.378 --> 11:11.605
[SPEAKER_01]: flash mob that I envisioned where all these skateboarders would come together dressed up like businessmen and businesswomen. And we would

11:11.645 --> 11:19.111
[SPEAKER_01]: take over the city of Toronto. Toronto, for your listeners that don't know, is like the New York City of Canada. It's like our business. So I

11:19.131 --> 11:26.958
[SPEAKER_01]: just thought it would be hilarious if everyone was congesting the city on skateboards wearing business attire. I still think it's hilarious.

11:27.896 --> 11:35.901
[SPEAKER_01]: And so I put up all these signs all over the city. I didn't know many longboarders at the time and longboarding was not popular back then,

11:36.081 --> 11:45.346
[SPEAKER_01]: not in Toronto anyway. And so I put up all these signs, I got on the radio a little bit and I was even threatened by the police. They told me like,

11:45.386 --> 11:51.650
[SPEAKER_01]: this is actually not a legal sport. You can't organize an event like this. You don't have a permit. And I was really nervous going into it. But

11:51.850 --> 12:02.432
[SPEAKER_01]: what I was really nervous about was not being thrown in jail or fined, it was actually that nobody would show up. And of all the reporters that I

12:02.833 --> 12:08.915
[SPEAKER_01]: had convinced to interview me so I could gain publicity for this project, people kept saying, hey, how many people do you expect? How

12:08.935 --> 12:15.478
[SPEAKER_01]: many people do you expect? I'd never done anything like this before in my life. And I realized that was the question that kept stressing me out.

12:15.998 --> 12:27.461
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was saying, I don't know, maybe 10, maybe 20, maybe 30, maybe 40. And at some point, I realized that I need to get rid of that expectation. How

12:27.481 --> 12:35.044
[SPEAKER_01]: many people are going to show up? And so people would ask me, how many people are going to show up? And I'd say, at least one. And that's okay,

12:35.424 --> 12:44.971
[SPEAKER_01]: because... That's the first year. This is the inaugural board meeting. Maybe only one person shows up. Maybe it's just me. But as long as I show

12:45.072 --> 12:53.179
[SPEAKER_01]: up, then the next year, maybe two people will show up. And then the next year, maybe four people will show up and then so on and so forth. As soon as

12:53.299 --> 13:02.245
[SPEAKER_01]: I got over that it could just be me and that would still be a successful event, the event would go on. I didn't worry anymore about how many

13:02.285 --> 13:09.275
[SPEAKER_01]: people would show up. And instead I could just put my energy into promoting it, to spreading the word and inviting people to come when I

13:09.315 --> 13:19.541
[SPEAKER_01]: saw them on the street, yada, yada. And so I, Long story short, 50 people showed up and that was mind blowing for me and for everybody involved.

13:19.841 --> 13:30.747
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody that rode long boards could have imagined 50 of them, of their peers all together for one event. And we had a great time. The next year, a

13:31.487 --> 13:39.792
[SPEAKER_01]: hundred people showed up. The next year, 150 people showed up. I only ran the event for three years. And then I left the city on that skateboarding

13:39.853 --> 13:47.334
[SPEAKER_01]: across Canada trip. But my peers continued to run the event and they continue to run it to this day. It would have just had its 21st

13:47.394 --> 13:58.316
[SPEAKER_01]: anniversary. And now something to the tune of two or 3,000 people show up, even if it's raining for this event. And it's important to recognize

13:58.376 --> 14:10.120
[SPEAKER_01]: that I would have... killed this baby before it was even born if I had continued to be so paralyzed by fear of that maybe nobody would show up.

14:10.980 --> 14:19.806
[SPEAKER_01]: And I appreciate this feels far away from paragliding because this is more to do with like the rejection of my peers and people saying, hey,

14:19.866 --> 14:30.272
[SPEAKER_01]: this is not a good idea. And some people did say that. But the bigger picture for me and what I learned from it was to not be concerned about how

14:30.352 --> 14:41.236
[SPEAKER_01]: it went, but just to try and to document whatever it is and to recognize that as valid. Because when you look back on it, big picture, 20 years,

14:41.756 --> 14:51.037
[SPEAKER_01]: hey, it would have been cool if I was the only person there. That would have been a real... I feel milestone because after 20 years, there would

14:51.057 --> 15:02.284
[SPEAKER_01]: still be 2000, 3000 people there. And so I feel like that, freeing myself of that expectation, not being worried about the numbers in this case,

15:02.324 --> 15:10.829
[SPEAKER_01]: which is really what matters for an event like this. I feel it was a big part of that and just embracing the idea of failure than that. And then

15:10.889 --> 15:23.935
[SPEAKER_01]: that idea of being okay with failure, has allowed me to plan these much, much more extravagant projects where it'd be very easy for me to fail in

15:24.035 --> 15:32.801
[SPEAKER_01]: one way or another. Got it. This does resonate with a lot of other kind of advice that some of the most esteemed guests and some brilliant pilots

15:32.841 --> 15:42.148
